body: "## Report and scope\n\nSource: https://github.com/unbraind/pm-cli/issues/1202\n\nReproduce receipt lifecycle after an aborted rebase and distinguish an abandoned candidate from a merge requiring review. Current source and Merge Safety documentation intentionally track privacy-safe durable sidecars; raw free-form retained/discarded values are clone-local under .git. Durable receipts retain hashes and only allowlisted low-cardinality scalar previews. The report has not demonstrated a secret leak. A blanket gitignore or evidence deletion would break cross-clone provenance and is not an accepted remedy.\n\n## Reported reproduction\n\n## Two defects, one reproduction\n\nRunning `git rebase`, hitting a conflict, and running `git rebase --abort` leaves behind merge-receipt state that outlives the operation it describes.\n\n## 1. The receipt survives the abort\n\nAfter `git rebase --abort`, the working tree is clean and the item is byte-identical to `HEAD`:\n\n```\n$ git diff --quiet HEAD -- .agents/pm/issues/pm-cli-website-nc0j.toon && echo identical\nidentical\n```\n\nNo merge landed. But a `state: \"pending\"` receipt persists, recording three conflicting fields (`acceptance_criteria`, `description`, `status`) with full `retained`/`discarded` values, and it is enough to fail the gate:\n\n```\n$ pm health --check-only\nok: False\nfailed_because: ['merge_decisions_unreviewed:1', 'merge_receipt_evidence_invalid:1']\n```\n\nSo an abandoned operation leaves a repository permanently red on a decision the operator explicitly abandoned. A receipt describes a merge that exists in no history and no working tree.\n\nExpected: receipts written by a merge driver during an operation that is subsequently aborted should not remain pending — either tied to the resulting commit, or discarded with the rest of the aborted state.\n\n## 2. The working-tree copy is not gitignored\n\nThe driver writes a second copy into the working tree, and it is untracked but **not ignored**:\n\n```\n$ git status --porcelain\n?? .agents/pm/merge-receipts/f9067de4-bc6c-477a-99de-c415951b2064.json\n\n$ git check-ignore -v .agents/pm/merge-receipts/f9067de4-...json\n(no match — not ignored)\n```\n\nThis is not a stale-template problem. A **fresh `pm init` on 2026.9.5** writes:\n\n```\n.agents/pm/runtime/\n.agents/pm/search/*\n.agents/pm/locks/\n.agents/pm/transactions/\n.agents/pm/checkpoints/\n!.agents/pm/search/eval-queries.json\n```\n\n`merge-receipts/` is absent. Checked across four independent repositories including a newly initialised one: zero have it ignored.\n\n### Why this matters\n\nThe documented convention for these trackers is to commit `.agents/pm/**` — every `.toon` together with its `history/*.jsonl`. An agent doing that with `git add -A` also commits the receipts. That:\n\n- publishes receipt bodies containing the **full retained and discarded text** of every conflicting field, into repositories that may be public, and\n- converts evidence the design treats as clone-local into tracked state, which changes what `merge_receipt_evidence_*` and the `tracked_runtime_cache_files` check mean. There is a guard for tracked runtime cache; there is none for tracked receipts.\n\nExpected: `pm init` ignores `.agents/pm/merge-receipts/` alongside `runtime/`, `locks/`, `transactions/` and `checkpoints/`, and existing repositories get the entry on the next write to that directory. If receipts are instead *meant* to be committed, then the health check that treats them as clone-local evidence and the `pm init` template disagree, and the intended contract should be stated.\n\n## Environment\n\n@unbrained/pm-cli 2026.9.4 (writer) and 2026.9.5 (reader and `pm init` probe), Linux, Node 22.\n\n## Delivery boundary\n\nIntake only in PR #1204. This item remains open and unclaimed; no implementation or reproduction success is asserted. Implement through the public SDK with thin CLI adapters and executable negative controls before closing."
